# Exercise: Combining Combinators
#
# The real power comes from combining dip, keep, and bi together.
#
# Pattern: compute multiple things from inputs without stack juggling.
#
# Example — given two numbers, produce both their sum AND their diff
# in one pass, without reshuffling:
#   ( 10 3 )
#   2dup i.+        — sum on top: ( 10 3 13 )
#   [ i.- ] dip     — hide 13, subtract underneath: ( 7 13 )
#
# Remember: dip hides the TOP value and runs the quotation on what's
# BELOW. So the trick is to compute one derived value first (while
# both inputs are still on the stack), then use dip to "reach under"
# that result and compute the other from the originals.
#
# Your task: Given two numbers on the stack, compute both their
# sum AND their product using combinators instead of stack juggling.
# Stack starts: ( 3 7 )
# Result: ( 10 21 ) — sum on bottom, product on top.
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# audit:allow(stub-lint-error)
# I AM NOT DONE

# Compute sum and product of two numbers using combinators
: sum-and-product ( Int Int -- Int Int )
    # Your code here. Given ( a b ), produce ( a+b a*b ).
    drop 0 0
;

: test-combine ( -- )
    3 7 sum-and-product

    # Do not edit below this line
    # Top should be 21 (product)
    21 test.assert-eq
    # Second should be 10 (sum)
    10 test.assert-eq
;
